+Feedback is a short report of records, each one sentence that points at its
+evidence instead of retelling it. Agents write the records in the closeout's
+**Feedback** field; a nightly collector files every report into the private
+feedback log (`BrianMills2718/agent-feedback-log`, one issue per report), and
+the loop later groups recurring problems, derives which claims are licensed
+by independent evidence, adopts rules or checks, and counts whether the
+failure stops (design: AES canonical `proposals/aes-learning-loop/PLAN.md`).
+Brian, 2026-10-07: report "as much information as it can provide and
+qualified and provenanced"; provenance should link "out to evidence [rather]
+than explains it or else it consumes my attention"; agents "dont always know
+the whole causal chain ... this is part of the post processing loop".
 
 ## Input
 
 `$ARGUMENTS` is the item text.
 
-**An empty invocation is a trigger, not a question.** It means: record what
-this session learned or hit and has not already recorded. Review the session
-for lessons, friction and problems, and file each un-recorded one (one issue
-per item). Do not ask the user what they learned -- they invoked this precisely
-so they would not have to write it themselves.
-
-Say so plainly and stop only when there is genuinely nothing to record: every
-candidate is already in the record, or the session learned nothing durable and
-hit nothing in its way. A session in which you corrected yourself, worked
-around a tool, or waited on something stalled is never in that state.
-Naming what you considered and rejected is more useful than an empty
-acknowledgement, and far more useful than a padded entry.
-
-## Step 0: Which kind?
-
-- **`kind:lesson`** — something learned: an observation true at the cited
-  evidence boundary (bug pattern, gotcha, tool behavior), an approach to use or
-  avoid, or both in one item. Corrections are lessons (Step 1).
-- **`kind:friction`** — a rule, tool, gate, hook, skill or instruction got in
-  the way: it was unclear, wrong, blocked the right action, or made it costly.
-  When a specific policy is involved, name its id in the body (`Policy: <id>`).
-- **`kind:problem`** — something failed and nobody was told, or a failure
-  outlived the run that hit it.
-
-A session that hit a blocked route, needed a workaround, corrected itself, or
-waited on something stalled has at least one item. Record each one.
-
-## Step 1: Always record a correction
-
-Before applying any skip rule below, check this first. **If you corrected a
-claim you had already made, or the user corrected a claim you asserted, record
-it as a practice-bearing learning.** This is not optional and does not depend
-on how the surrounding work turns out.
-
-A correction is durable the moment it happens. Whether the substantive question
-is still open is a separate matter, and deferring the one does not license
-dropping the other. When the correction establishes both what was wrong and how
-to avoid repeating it, use both `observation` and `practice` on one entry. If the
-domain observation is genuinely unsettled, record only the proven practice now
-and the observation later. Do not write the correction into a
-Concerns line and move on — a closing report is read once and discarded, and
-that is where corrections go to die.
-
-Record it as `kind:lesson`, with `recommended_action` naming what to do
-instead and the body naming the reasoning that produced the error, not the
-topic it was about. "Asserted a count from a status field without checking what
-the field measures" is a reasoning; "Repository inventory" is a topic.
-
-The bar is whether it happened, not whether it feels significant. An agent is a
-poor judge of which of its own errors will recur, and the corpus shows the same
-few reasoning failures repeating across unrelated projects for months.
-
-## Step 2: Check it is worth recording
-
-Skip, and say so, when the input is:
-
-- already recorded by the repository — code structure, git history, or a fix
-  visible in the diff;
-- session bookkeeping, status, or narration;
-- a restatement of the task rather than something learned or hit;
-- already an open or closed issue in the record. Search first:
-  `gh issue list --repo BrianMills2718/agentic-engineering-system-canonical --state all --search "<keywords>" --limit 10`,
-  and grep the legacy registers (`project-meta/learnings/entries/`,
-  `project-meta/learnings.md`, `project-meta/policy_friction.md`). If the same
-  item exists, add a comment to the open issue with this occurrence (a second
-  independent instance is the signal the weekly run counts) instead of filing a
-  duplicate.
-
-**A decline must name what it defers to.** Cite the issue number, legacy entry
-ID, commit, or memory slug. "Already recorded" with no reference is not a
-reason. Measured across 296 closing reports: of 55 declines given on those
-grounds, the 49% that cited something checkable all verified, and the other 51%
-cited nothing at all.
-
-## Step 3: File
-
-Title starts with `Lesson:`, `Friction:` or `Problem:` and says the item in
-one plain sentence. The body opens with three lines, then what happened:
